result.summary:
Distance and volatility dominate which liquidity is taken next. ICT labels are only small modifiers on that baseline. A distance-matched test on 2 years of 1H data across 8 markets found equal highs/lows taken 63-82% of the time within 24 bars, against 68-76% for ordinary swings, and the "equal" label added less than 1 pp beyond distance. FVGs react only about 5 pp above a distance-matched random level (about 40,000 events). So the engine's first-passage/empirical PoT design with a distance-matched ledger baseline is the right architecture, and ICT features should be bounded multipliers learned per chart.

After liquidity is taken, continuation is more common than reversal:
- an SSRN walk-forward on G10 FX found swept levels predict continuation in 65 of 66 fold-selections;
- reversal after a sweep occurred only 39-44% of the time in the distance-matched test;
- on YM, a PDH break gave a green session 67-81% of the time;
- Osler's RBS order data shows price accelerating for up to about 2 hours after crossing stop clusters.

The engine hard-codes "close back inside = reversal" (line 1581) and should learn that sign per chart. The strongest evidence in the domain (Osler, J. Finance 2003; Kavajecz & Odders-White, RFS 2004) is that stops sit just beyond obvious levels and limit orders sit at them: 14.4% of stop-buys are in the band just above a 00 level versus 7.4% just below, and support/resistance coincides with order-book depth peaks. The engine has no round-number awareness at all.

Peer-reviewed work also shows that each prior bounce raises the bounce probability on the next approach, and that this decays within hours. The engine rewards tests as extra liquidity magnitude, which may be the wrong sign for a strict trade-through hit within H bars.

ICT's own selection rules are codeable but none has been independently validated:
- low-hanging fruit first;
- the IRL/ERL alternation;
- turtle soup conditioned on premium/discount;
- high- vs low-resistance runs;
- "priming" of relative equal highs;
- an MSS counts only after a purge;
- PDH/PDL close rules for daily bias.

The top proposal is a candidate (shadow) ledger that grades every pool level against the distance-matched baseline and learns a bounded lift per ICT feature. That turns each ICT claim into something the chart measures and shows, and makes the engine's selection edge visible. It is followed by a post-sweep outcome learner that feeds the draw vector, and by round-number stop-depth/confluence.

The question of which side gets taken first is almost entirely range geometry. Across 17 dataset and reference combinations, the parameter-free gambler's-ruin rule P(prior high first) = (C−L1)/(H1−L1) predicts the first-taken previous-candle extreme 70-81% of the time. The datasets were S&P 500 and NASDAQ daily 1999-2018, GOOG, their weekly aggregates, EURUSD 1H/4H, and NQ/GC/EURUSD 1H 2024-26; all numbers are reproducible from study/bias_tools. The ICT daily-bias rule (close through PDH → PDH next; sweep and close back inside → opposite side) scores 69-76%. A volatility-preserving shape-shuffle null scores the same 68-76% (difference −2.2 to +1.9 pp on 13 datasets). Used as a hard rule it is worse than geometry, at 64-73%. CRT targets are reached before the sweep extreme only 39-48% of the time, again equal to the null. An independent 4,200-variant CRT backtest on NQ tied a coin-flip placebo, and an NQ AMD/Power-of-3 study (N=484) found no directional information beyond the daily trend regime. Structure bias, EMA trend regime, weekly-open and midnight-open bias, Turtle Soup and N-bar breakouts add at most about ±2-4 pp. Their sign flips with asset and timescale: daily equities and hourly FX mean-revert, while NQ and gold intraday are calibrated to geometry. So they must be learned per chart, never hard-coded. The engine currently picks the side with a hard-coded structure/displacement/raid vote D (gate 0.35, ×0.5 counter-draw, 30% weight). The sibling study measured that vote at 0.49-0.54, the same as random-walk controls. In an engine-like simulation, choosing the side by such a vote instead of by race geometry cost 4-10 pp of which-side-first accuracy and 2-7 pp of hit-within-H on all 5 datasets tested. The core recommendation is to keep the draw-side-first architecture but redefine D as a calibrated P(best buy-side finalist reached before best sell-side finalist). Its prior is the engine's own f_race. Every bias feature (structure, raids, previous-candle class, premium/discount, CRT) can move it only through weights learned online and scored prequentially against geometry in a new side ledger (Brier skill), which makes every bias claim self-measuring on the trader's chart. The secondary recommendations are:
- an honest per-timeframe daily-bias readout (P(prior high first / prior low first / neither), with the CRT or close-through class and its measured lift);
- setting the unsupported "rejected sweep = reversal" sign to neutral;
- showing measured skill in the bias table;
- choosing the PoT surface's regime split by held-out score.

concepts.definition:
Bullish if C1>H2, or if L1<L2 and C1≥L2; bearish mirrors; otherwise neutral. The same rule applies on weekly bars for PWH/PWL. Variants:
- session-based PDH/PDL (NY RTH) vs the 18:00 or midnight day;
- TTrades: continuation days respect the previous range's 50% before taking PDH.

Candle 1 = range (high, low, 50%). Candle 2 trades beyond one extreme and closes back inside; a close beyond voids it as a breakout. Candle 3 targets the opposite extreme (sometimes the 50% first). Variants:
- strict vs flexible (extra inside candles, confirmation candle);
- HTF range candle with LTF MSS/FVG entry;
- 'CRT into an HTF key level' rated stronger, 'in open space' weak.

concepts.evidenceNotes:
[this study] The target is hit before the sweep extreme in 39-48% of decided cases (S&P D 0.458 vs null 0.458; NQ 1H 0.430 vs 0.411; GC 4bar 0.388 vs 0.425). It is reached at all within 3 candles 45-62% of the time, no better than the null. On equity indices the swept high is retaken more than the null (S&P D 0.691 vs 0.639). External:
- a 4,200-variant CRT backtest on NQ 2021-26: survivors tied a coin-flip placebo (Strange Labs);
- practitioner claim of a 45-50% raw win rate (tradingwyckoff).

concepts.definition:
Engine definition:
- D = clamp((wS·sV + wM·mV + wL·liqEvt)/(wS+wM+wL)) with sV = 0.5·htfBias + 0.3·stfBias + 0.2·exeIB;
- mV = displacement direction with half-life 12;
- liqEvt = decayed raid outcomes;
- side gate |D| ≥ 0.35.
ICT order flow: HTF candles respect bullish PD arrays, with bodies closing beyond the CE.

concepts.evidenceNotes:
[this study, turtle.py and cells.py] 20-bar breakout excess vs geometry: daily equities −6 to −9 pp, EURUSD 1H −5 to −9 pp, NQ/GC intraday +0 to +5.5 pp. External:
- SPY ρ(1) = −0.081, magnitude-driven, with mean reversion confined to equities and bonds (arXiv 2606.29591);
- trending regime from hours to years (arXiv 2501.16772);
- time-series momentum (Moskowitz, Ooi & Pedersen 2012, strong, monthly horizons).

arXiv 2610.35887 (NQ 2022-26, N=484 signal days):
- the signal fires on 44% of days;
- 57% of targets are delivered before the 09:40 entry;
- no directional information beyond the trend regime;
- after London sweeps the Asia high in a bull market, price closes above the Asia midpoint 75.2% of the time.

